I will go through src/paginate.ts in the order a request hits it and change one thing at a time.

The smallest change that fixes it:

```diff
-  const page = Number(url.searchParams.get("page") ?? "1");
-  const pageSize = Number(url.searchParams.get("pageSize") ?? "20");
+  const limit = parseLimit(url.searchParams.get("limit"));
+  const cursor = url.searchParams.get("cursor");
```

The smallest change that fixes it:

```diff
-  hasNext: page <= totalPages,
+  hasNext: page < totalPages,
+  nextCursor: page < totalPages ? encodeCursor(page + 1) : null,
```

Nothing here needs a new dependency, so the change stays inside the module. I avoided a second pass over the list; the index is enough to find where the page starts. I moved the magic number into a named constant so the next reader does not have to guess its unit. The diff is deliberately small: one branch disappears, one helper appears, and the call sites stay readable. I moved the magic number into a named constant so the next reader does not have to guess its unit.

Here is the edit:

```diff
-  const end = start + pageSize + 1;
-  items: all.slice(start, end),
+  const end = Math.min(totalItems, start + pageSize);
+  items: all.slice(start, end),
```

The failure I am fixing is the kind that only shows up on the second page, which is why the tests missed it. The tests I am adding cover the first page, the last page and a page past the end.

The empty list is the case to check first: it must produce an empty page and a null cursor, not a crash. I checked the behaviour with an empty string, a null value and a number before settling on the guard. The tests I am adding cover the first page, the last page and a page past the end. The simplest fix is to compute the rule once, in a helper, and let both call sites use it.

A single item and an exact multiple of the page size are the two edges worth pinning down. The rounding happens once, at the end, so the per-line totals still add up to the invoice total.

```ts
export interface Page<T> {
  items: T[];
  nextCursor: string | null;
  limit: number;
}

export function toPage<T>(items: T[], decode: (item: T) => string, limit: number): Page<T> {
  const next = items.length > limit ? decode(items[limit] as T) : null;
  return { items: items.slice(0, limit), nextCursor: next, limit };
}
```

There is one more caller in the export path that repeats the same three lines, so it gets the helper too. Every branch is covered by a test now, including the one that returns the empty page. I did not touch the response shape, because the client already depends on every field in it. The simplest fix is to compute the rule once, in a helper, and let both call sites use it.

The change itself:

```diff
-const date = new Date(day);
+const date = new Date(`${day}T00:00:00Z`);
```

The old code copied the whole array on every call; the new one copies only the page. The status codes stay the same, so no client has to change how it reads the error. I did not touch the response shape, because the client already depends on every field in it.

